 **Formulas:**
-
-1.  **Rotation Matrix from Quaternion:**
-    Given quaternion $(r, x, y, z)$ (where $r$ is the scalar part, often $w$):
-    $$
-    R = \begin{bmatrix}
-    1 - 2(y^2 + z^2) & 2(xy - rz) & 2(xz + ry) \\
-    2(xy + rz) & 1 - 2(x^2 + z^2) & 2(yz - rx) \\
-    2(xz - ry) & 2(yz + rx) & 1 - 2(x^2 + y^2)
-    \end{bmatrix}
-    $$
-    [S1, unofficial]
-
-2.  **3D Covariance Matrix:**
-    Let $S$ be a diagonal matrix with entries $\exp(\text{log\_scale}) \cdot \text{scale\_modifier}$.
-    Let $R$ be the rotation matrix derived from the quaternion.
-    $$ M = S \cdot R $$
-    $$ \Sigma = M \cdot M^T $$
-    The upper triangle of $\Sigma$ is extracted: $[\Sigma_{00}, \Sigma_{01}, \Sigma_{02}, \Sigma_{11}, \Sigma_{12}, \Sigma_{22}]$.
-    [S1, unofficial]
-
-3.  **2D Covariance Projection:**
-    Transform position to view space: $t = \text{viewMatrix} \cdot [x, y, z, 1]^T$.
-    Clamp ratios to handle frustum edges:
-    $$ l_x = \min(\max(t.x/t.z, -1.3 \cdot \tan(\text{fov}_x)), 1.3 \cdot \tan(\text{fov}_x)) $$
-    $$ l_y = \min(\max(t.y/t.z, -1.3 \cdot \tan(\text{fov}_y)), 1.3 \cdot \tan(\text{fov}_y)) $$
-    Compute Jacobian $J$:
-    $$ J = \begin{bmatrix}
-    f_x/t.z & 0 & -f_x \cdot t.x / t.z^2 \\
-    0 & f_y/t.z & -f_y \cdot t.y / t.z^2
-    \end{bmatrix} $$
-    Compute $T = \text{viewMatrix}^T \cdot J$ (taking the relevant 3x2 or 2x3 sub-blocks as appropriate for the dimensionality of the covariance multiplication).
-    Compute 2D covariance:
-    $$ \Sigma_{2D} = T^T \cdot \Sigma \cdot T $$
-    Apply low-pass filter to ensure minimum size:
-    $$ \Sigma_{2D}[0][0] += 0.3 $$
-    $$ \Sigma_{2D}[1][1] += 0.3 $$
-    Return the upper triangle: $[\Sigma_{2D}[0][0], \Sigma_{2D}[0][1], \Sigma_{2D}[1][1]]$.
-    [S1, unofficial]
-
-4.  **Conic Matrix and Radius:**
-    Let $\Sigma_{2D} = \begin{bmatrix} a & b \\ b & c \end{bmatrix}$.
-    $$ \text{det} = a \cdot c - b^2 $$
-    $$ \text{det\_inv} = 1.0 / \text{det} $$
-    $$ \text{conic} = [c \cdot \text{det\_inv}, -b \cdot \text{det\_inv}, a \cdot \text{det\_inv}] $$
-    $$ \text{mid} = 0.5 \cdot (a + c) $$
-    $$ \lambda_1 = \text{mid} + \sqrt{\max(0.1, \text{mid}^2 - \text{det})} $$
-    $$ \lambda_2 = \text{mid} - \sqrt{\max(0.1, \text{mid}^2 - \text{det})} $$
-    $$ \text{radius\_px} = \lceil 3.0 \cdot \sqrt{\max(\lambda_1, \lambda_2)} \rceil $$
-    $$ \text{radius\_ndc} = [\text{radius\_px} / \text{canvas\_height}, \text{radius\_px} / \text{canvas\_width}] $$
-    [S1, unofficial]
-
-5.  **Fragment Shader Power Function:**
-    Let $d = [u, v]$ be the distance from the Gaussian center in UV space.
-    Let $\text{conic} = [A, B, C]$ (inverse of 2D covariance upper triangle).
-    $$ \text{power} = -0.5 \cdot (A \cdot d.x^2 + C \cdot d.y^2) + B \cdot d.x \cdot d.y $$
-    [S1, unofficial]
+1.  **Rotation Matrix from Quaternion:** Given quaternion $(r, x, y, z)$:
+    $$ R = \begin{bmatrix} 1 - 2(y^2 + z^2) & 2(xy - rz) & 2(xz + ry) \\ 2(xy + rz) & 1 - 2(x^2 + z^2) & 2(yz - rx) \\ 2(xz - ry) & 2(yz + rx) & 1 - 2(x^2 + y^2) \end{bmatrix} $$ [S1, unofficial]
+2.  **3D Covariance:** Let $S$ be a diagonal matrix with entries $\exp(\text{log\_scale}) \cdot \text{scale\_modifier}$. Let $R$ be the rotation matrix. $M = S \cdot R$. $\Sigma = M^T \cdot M$. Extract the upper triangle: $[\Sigma_{00}, \Sigma_{01}, \Sigma_{02}, \Sigma_{11}, \Sigma_{12}, \Sigma_{22}]$ [S1, unofficial].
+3.  **2D Covariance Projection:** Transform position to view space: $t = \text{viewMatrix} \cdot [x, y, z, 1]^T$. Clamp ratios to handle frustum edges: $l_x = \text{clamp}(t.x/t.z, -1.3 \cdot \tan(\text{fov}_x), 1.3 \cdot \tan(\text{fov}_x))$, $l_y = \text{clamp}(t.y/t.z, -1.3 \cdot \tan(\text{fov}_y), 1.3 \cdot \tan(\text{fov}_y))$. Compute Jacobian $J$: $J_{00} = f_x/t.z$, $J_{02} = -f_x \cdot t.x / t.z^2$, $J_{11} = f_y/t.z$, $J_{12} = -f_y \cdot t.y / t.z^2$. Compute $T = \text{viewMatrix}^T \cdot J$. Compute 2D covariance: $\Sigma_{2D} = T^T \cdot \Sigma \cdot T$. Apply low-pass filter to ensure minimum size: $\Sigma_{2D}[0][0] += 0.3$, $\Sigma_{2D}[1][1] += 0.3$. Return the upper triangle: $[\Sigma_{2D}[0][0], \Sigma_{2D}[0][1], \Sigma_{2D}[1][1]]$ [S1, unofficial].
+4.  **Conic Matrix and Radius:** Let $\Sigma_{2D} = \begin{bmatrix} a & b \\ b & c \end{bmatrix}$. $\text{det} = a \cdot c - b^2$. $\text{det\_inv} = 1.0 / \text{det}$. $\text{conic} = [c \cdot \text{det\_inv}, -b \cdot \text{det\_inv}, a \cdot \text{det\_inv}]$. $\text{mid} = 0.5 \cdot (a + c)$. $\lambda_1 = \text{mid} + \sqrt{\max(0.1, \text{mid}^2 - \text{det})}$, $\lambda_2 = \text{mid} - \sqrt{\max(0.1, \text{mid}^2 - \text{det})}$. $\text{radius\_px} = \lceil 3.0 \cdot \sqrt{\max(\lambda_1, \lambda_2)} \rceil$. $\text{radius\_ndc} = [\text{radius\_px} / \text{canvas\_height}, \text{radius\_px} / \text{canvas\_width}]$ [S1, unofficial].
+5.  **Fragment Shader Power Function:** Let $d = -\text{input.uv}$. Let $\text{conic} = [A, B, C]$. $\text{power} = -0.5 \cdot (A \cdot d.x^2 + C \cdot d.y^2) + B \cdot d.x \cdot d.y$. Discard fragment if $\text{power} > 0.0$. Compute $\alpha = \min(0.99, \text{opacity} \cdot \exp(\text{power}))$ [S1, unofficial].
 
